WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.500
Hello and welcome to an introductory
organic video focusing on

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.710
addition reactions of alkenes.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:10.110
So in this video, we're gonna go
through all the different types of

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:13.710
addition reactions that you typically
see in the introductory organic

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.540
course, focusing first on alkenes.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.180
The whole idea about this with addition is
that we are going to be adding something,

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:26.550
but one way for us to be able to look
at this reaction is imagine whenever we

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.710
have an Quin, how it has the double bond.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.730
Well, we want to imagine that
double bond as a set of doors that

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:36.690
we're gonna be opening up to add
our different components to it.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:42.960
So when I say open the cabinet doors, we
want to be able to redraw our structure

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.420
and to add our components to it.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:50.519
So with all these different reactions,
the big thing about them is generally the

00:00:50.519 --> 00:00:53.490
carbon structure always stays the same.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:58.379
Uh, we just bring along all the
little hydrogens and components.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.059
Now one other thing.

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:01.650
What are we exactly looking at?

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:02.670
What are we adding?

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:06.960
Well, in this particular reaction,
I'm showing just X as a species.

00:01:07.289 --> 00:01:09.720
It doesn't necessarily matter what it is.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.190
We are adding X.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:16.080
Now, as I mentioned, we are opening
up the cabinet door so that double

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:22.200
bond opens and we are going to be
adding an X species to both sides.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.650
So we have X on the left
and then X on the right.

00:01:26.039 --> 00:01:28.830
And that's the whole idea is
we're opening up the door and

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.300
then adding different species.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.270
So we're gonna go through a bunch
of different reactions to understand

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.330
that when you have this, we are always
gonna be starting with an alkene

00:01:38.429 --> 00:01:43.440
on the left, making an alkane as a
product, that we go from that double

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.429
bond CC to a single bonded C over here.

00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:51.120
And the whole idea is that when we
write this, we're gonna be adding two

00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:53.970
components together to make one product.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:55.320
So we go from two things.

00:01:55.649 --> 00:01:58.110
Down to one product in the end.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.750
So let's take a look
at our first reaction.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:10.440
So addition reactions generally
always have in the name what we

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.780
are adding for this first reaction.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:17.070
We have hydrogenation, which
is all about adding hydrogen.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.609
We can see that directly
in the name hydrogen.

00:02:21.135 --> 00:02:23.205
So with this one, how is it written?

00:02:23.205 --> 00:02:24.825
Well, what we can see is here.

00:02:24.945 --> 00:02:29.325
Each two is written on top of our
reaction arrow, letting you know this

00:02:29.325 --> 00:02:30.675
is what we're going to be adding.

00:02:31.545 --> 00:02:36.105
Most of these reactions will have a
catalyst or, uh, in this one specific for

00:02:36.105 --> 00:02:38.025
hydrogenation, and it's written below.

00:02:38.025 --> 00:02:41.985
The arrow let you know that there
is a catalyst that is present in

00:02:41.985 --> 00:02:43.545
order for the reaction to happen.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:48.210
Now, as I mentioned before, this entire
reaction is gonna be happening where

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:50.340
we're opening up those double doors.

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:55.260
So I'm gonna just kind of draw that, that
double bond and just highlight where the

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.270
doors are, where they're gonna be opening.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.730
So first thing that we're gonna be doing
is re-drawing our structure so we have

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:08.430
that, those three individual carbons,
and then we're gonna be opening up the

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:12.030
double door and they don't necessarily
have to be pointing the same direction.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.750
We are opening it to show that
we are going to be adding a

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.960
hydrogen to each component.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.390
So whenever we have H2, that is
telling you that we have a hydrogen

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:29.460
being added to both sides of the
double bond as we are opening that up.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:34.230
So as we can see, one gets, one
hydrogen gets added on, one carbon,

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:39.690
one on the other, which is where our
locations of our double bond were.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:42.420
We add a hydrogen to both parts.

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:44.790
Let's try another example.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:51.720
So in fact, we have multiple, so if we are
to do these individual reactions, the same

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.440
idea is we redraw the carbon structure.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.860
So we'll redraw our shape
here for the first structure.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:02.280
And what we are gonna be
doing is we're opening up the

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.020
location of the double bond.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.890
So the double bond here is on
carbon two, and then carbon three.

00:04:08.535 --> 00:04:12.255
So we open it up there and
we're gonna be adding a hydrogen

00:04:12.375 --> 00:04:14.805
onto each individual part.

00:04:15.765 --> 00:04:17.084
Same thing with the next chain.

00:04:17.475 --> 00:04:22.905
Uh, with this ring structure, we're going
to draw our ring, we're going to open up

00:04:22.905 --> 00:04:29.265
the double bond, and we'll add a hydrogen
to each part so we can see that we're

00:04:29.265 --> 00:04:32.085
going from two products down to one.

00:04:33.045 --> 00:04:35.805
Even with this last example, we'll redraw.

00:04:36.315 --> 00:04:37.425
The carbon framework.

00:04:37.425 --> 00:04:42.855
We'll even put the halogen in the bromine
and the chlorine, and it's the same idea,

00:04:43.155 --> 00:04:47.595
is that we are going to be opening up
that double bond and just highlighting

00:04:47.595 --> 00:04:48.975
all these, just so we can see it.

00:04:49.935 --> 00:04:54.555
We're gonna open that up and put a
hydrogen on each individual part to

00:04:54.555 --> 00:04:57.225
show how the doors are opening, right?

00:04:57.225 --> 00:05:02.865
We're adding a car, adding our group
onto the carbons, participating.

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:04.170
In the double bond.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:07.920
So whichever structure we're looking
at, whether it's the first one, right?

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.490
We have carbon number two,
and then carbon number three.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:15.630
Those are going to be
participating in that opening.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.030
Let's go onto our next reaction.

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:22.350
Our next reaction type is
Halogenation, and from this one you

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:24.240
can see the same general premise.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:28.890
It gives you the name inside
of saying -ation, right?

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:29.940
We're adding something.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:31.770
So in this case, we're adding a halogen.

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:37.560
So halogen can be chlorine,
flourine, iodine, bromine any

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.300
of those individual species.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:43.890
So usually we denote that with the
letter X and that can represent

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:48.050
any of these major halogens.

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:52.020
So for this one, it's gonna follow
the same idea of hydrogenation.

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:56.820
We open up the double bond and
we will have a group on each

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:58.890
of those individual carbons.

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:01.320
So just to kind of label
those carbons, right?

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:05.340
So we have one carbon here in red,
the other one in red as well, and

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:09.659
we can really illustrate that that
is what's going to be opening.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:12.659
So we will redraw our structure.

00:06:14.805 --> 00:06:17.445
And we are going to open
up the double bonds.

00:06:17.445 --> 00:06:20.534
So we'll put one on carbon
one, one on carbon two.

00:06:21.015 --> 00:06:25.135
And being that we're adding
chlorines, there will be a Cl

00:06:25.155 --> 00:06:27.224
on each individual structure.

00:06:27.645 --> 00:06:30.765
Um, so we're adding to each part
and it might be a common thing

00:06:30.765 --> 00:06:33.645
where you even might have to name
it and go through naming rules or

00:06:33.645 --> 00:06:36.104
an even go farther so this video.

00:06:36.104 --> 00:06:37.604
We're just gonna particularly show.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.230
Is how we can add these little parts.

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:44.280
And if you want to, you can always try
to clean up your structure as well.

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:48.360
So for example, we can show that,
okay, we're adding our little

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:52.620
chlorine, chlorine with the methyl
group, so it looks a little bit more

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:54.390
like a correct geometry as well.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:56.640
Let's try and let's try some more.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:00.240
So with these particular
geometries, same idea.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:03.990
We're going to be copying
down the main structure.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:06.840
So here we have our chain.

00:07:07.395 --> 00:07:11.745
And as before, like I mentioned,
we have our double bond.

00:07:12.015 --> 00:07:14.625
So this is our door that
we're gonna be opening.

00:07:15.105 --> 00:07:20.955
So we will add one on this carbon and
then one on the next carbon to show.

00:07:21.284 --> 00:07:25.275
Okay, we open the door and we're
adding a bromine to each portion.

00:07:26.205 --> 00:07:30.914
For the next one, we are adding
iodine to our cyclohexene.

00:07:31.469 --> 00:07:36.479
So in this case, what we're going to be
doing is we are going to be adding our

00:07:36.479 --> 00:07:39.419
cyclohexene to our double bond here.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:45.180
We are going to be opening up that
structure and then writing an iodine

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:48.900
on each portion of this species.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:56.760
And notice how it went from an alkene
to an a cycloalkane with halides on it.

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:59.099
So it's an alkyl halide.

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:03.900
That we're changing the
functional group along with it.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:08.670
So the carbon chain always stays the same
as the functional group that is changing.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:11.700
So same thing with this last example.

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:14.310
We are gonna be opening
up our double bond.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:19.710
We will leave our bromine and our
chlorine that was originally there, and

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:25.590
we're gonna be adding chlorines to each
individual part of the double bond.

00:08:26.115 --> 00:08:29.925
So we're gonna open up those
doors and add our pieces.

00:08:30.435 --> 00:08:33.435
And this is the whole idea
with these types of reactions

00:08:33.435 --> 00:08:35.685
that involve the same species.

00:08:35.685 --> 00:08:38.935
So we have H2 or F2 or Cl2

00:08:39.284 --> 00:08:42.135
they all have that
diatomic characteristic.

00:08:42.615 --> 00:08:45.885
Now let's go into something
that is not a diatomic and is

00:08:45.885 --> 00:08:48.195
more of a heterogeneous species.

00:08:48.525 --> 00:08:51.825
So hydro halogenation is
when you add a hydrogen.

00:08:52.335 --> 00:08:57.194
And a halogen, meaning we have two
different species, but it's still gonna

00:08:57.194 --> 00:08:59.745
be following the same general idea.

00:08:59.745 --> 00:09:01.935
We're gonna be adding both components.

00:09:02.535 --> 00:09:05.805
However, one thing you're gonna
quickly realize is, well, if I'm

00:09:05.805 --> 00:09:10.305
adding two pieces, so we have, we're
gonna be adding a hydrogen, um,

00:09:10.305 --> 00:09:11.835
and why don't we color code these?

00:09:11.835 --> 00:09:18.465
So let's say if we add a hydrogen in
blue and we'll add a bromine in red.

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:24.390
Well, the same idea is that we're gonna
be opening up the double bond, however,

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:27.660
what side do you put each species on?

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:32.220
Because if we are to draw this
structure, we can see that.

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:32.610
All right.

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:37.050
Well, we'll have one species where
we can put the hydrogen, let's

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:42.000
say, or the bromine, let's say on
the left, hydrogen on the right.

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:45.150
And if we were to redraw
that, we could also write it.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:51.150
Where I could have hydrogen on
the left and bromine on the right.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:55.560
Now the question is,
well, which one is true?

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:58.710
We have two different options of products.

00:09:59.175 --> 00:10:02.745
So what we actually have to do
is we have to be able to predict

00:10:02.835 --> 00:10:07.485
the correct or the major product
that is formed within a reaction.

00:10:07.875 --> 00:10:09.975
So how exactly do we do that?

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:12.795
Well, we're going to go
off the following rule.

00:10:13.095 --> 00:10:17.145
The following rule is all about,
it's called Markovnikov's rule.

00:10:17.715 --> 00:10:20.745
This is one, it's one of our
few rules that we come across

00:10:20.745 --> 00:10:24.795
in introductory organic, which
is a a scary long Russian name.

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:29.820
So with Markovnikov's rule, the
whole idea behind it is that

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:33.660
hydrogen wants to bond near hydrogen.

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:38.070
So the way I like to view it is
that hydrogen wants to make more

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:40.440
friends that are just like hydrogen.

00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:45.210
Hydrogen is a social butterfly that
likes to bond to another species.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:50.970
So if we have our example from before,
well, we had two different products.

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:54.450
How did we know which
one is the major species?

00:10:54.824 --> 00:10:58.694
So what it comes down to is
going to our original reactant.

00:10:59.265 --> 00:11:03.824
So if we go to this reactant, we
are looking at the double bond.

00:11:04.064 --> 00:11:08.055
And like I mentioned, hydrogen
wants to bond near more hydrogens.

00:11:08.505 --> 00:11:11.025
So what we're gonna do is we're
gonna look at both carbons, the

00:11:11.025 --> 00:11:15.015
one on the left and the one on
the right and count our hydrogens.

00:11:15.584 --> 00:11:17.775
So how exactly do we do that?

00:11:17.775 --> 00:11:19.064
Well, let's take a look.

00:11:19.395 --> 00:11:24.330
So if I'm on the carbon right
here, How exactly do I go

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:26.580
about trying to count carbons?

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:32.430
And let's pick a different color
other than R, other than yellow.

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:33.630
So let's redraw that.

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:40.140
So if we have, let's say our carbon
right here, how can we decide that?

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:44.340
Well, we have one bond here, two bonds.

00:11:44.969 --> 00:11:48.239
And then three bonds
all around this carbon.

00:11:48.599 --> 00:11:50.250
So how much room is there for hydrogen?

00:11:50.339 --> 00:11:56.459
Well, we can fit a total of one
more hydrogen on this carbon.

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:01.560
Now if we look at the other carbon,
which I'll draw in purple here.

00:12:02.069 --> 00:12:04.530
Well, what can we do
if we zoom in on that?

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:08.729
How many bonds are around
that particular carbon?

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:11.250
Well, same idea as before.

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:15.120
We're going to count the number
of bonds it currently has, and

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:16.560
we can see that we have one.

00:12:16.949 --> 00:12:21.510
We have two for our double bond,
three and four for the single bond.

00:12:21.959 --> 00:12:24.150
So is there any room for hydrogen?

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:27.209
And if you remember, carbon
can bond up to four things.

00:12:27.689 --> 00:12:30.030
It already has all four bonds.

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:37.079
So for this one, well, we have
zero hydrogens on this carbon.

00:12:37.635 --> 00:12:43.724
We have zero on this side and then
we have one hydrogen on the left.

00:12:44.324 --> 00:12:49.604
So what is gonna happen is hydrogen
wants to be with other hydrogens.

00:12:50.025 --> 00:12:56.354
So the correct structure is gonna be this
bottom one, which showed that hydrogen

00:12:56.415 --> 00:12:59.025
wants to be with its fellow hydrogens.

00:12:59.505 --> 00:13:01.185
So both of them are gonna be there.

00:13:01.214 --> 00:13:03.015
It is happy it has its friend.

00:13:03.435 --> 00:13:07.005
So this is what we refer
to as the major product.

00:13:07.515 --> 00:13:11.715
When it comes to the markovnikov
type of reaction, let's look at

00:13:11.715 --> 00:13:13.665
some further examples of this.

00:13:13.815 --> 00:13:18.375
With this example, we're gonna be taking
the following alkane and adding HCl to it.

00:13:18.645 --> 00:13:20.505
So what do we have to exactly do?

00:13:20.835 --> 00:13:24.225
Well, it's the same idea as our
hydrogenation and halogenation.

00:13:24.345 --> 00:13:28.275
We just have an extra rule to tell
us which product is the major one.

00:13:28.695 --> 00:13:30.735
So we're still gonna be
opening up our doors.

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:33.420
Around our double bond.

00:13:33.689 --> 00:13:38.100
So let's highlight the double bond to show
where everything is gonna be happening.

00:13:38.730 --> 00:13:42.360
Now with this structure, what we have
to do is we need to count the number

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:47.310
of hydrogens on the right carbon as
well as the one on the left carbon.

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:53.205
So if we are to look closely well
on our structure, If we are to

00:13:53.205 --> 00:13:59.205
look on the left hand side, well,
how many hydrogens do you have?

00:13:59.265 --> 00:14:03.705
Well, what you have to do is count all
of the bonds that are currently there

00:14:03.735 --> 00:14:06.075
and we can see that we have one, two.

00:14:06.555 --> 00:14:08.715
So remember, every carbon needs four.

00:14:09.015 --> 00:14:12.135
So there are two hydrogens on the left.

00:14:12.135 --> 00:14:16.665
One, we're gonna do the same exact
thing on the right hand side.

00:14:17.145 --> 00:14:20.415
So when we do that, we are
counting our number of bonds

00:14:20.415 --> 00:14:21.345
and we can see that we have.

00:14:21.839 --> 00:14:26.640
1, 2, 3, 4, all around this.

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:30.480
So there is zero hydrogens on the right.

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:35.010
So when we open up our doors,
which side is it gonna be going on?

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:39.329
We can see that's going on the
left hand side of our door.

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:44.010
So we are going to redraw
our structure, right?

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:50.699
So we will have that just like, so
we're going to open up both sides.

00:14:51.944 --> 00:14:57.285
So we're opening up those doors and
hydrogen is gonna go on the right hand

00:14:57.285 --> 00:15:02.535
side with all of the other hydrogens
that are a part of the structure.

00:15:03.074 --> 00:15:06.915
But let's show that and maybe in blue
just to kind of show that we have the

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:13.694
two original ones and then chlorine
is going to go on the right hand side.

00:15:14.114 --> 00:15:18.525
So hydrogen went with its other hydrogen
friends when we opened the door.

00:15:19.305 --> 00:15:23.265
Let's go on to another reaction
type that you're going to see.

00:15:23.505 --> 00:15:25.875
The last reaction that we're
gonna come across in addition

00:15:25.875 --> 00:15:28.485
reactions is the hydration reaction.

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:31.680
And what you can get is, well,
what is it when you hydrate is

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:33.180
you are adding water, right?

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:34.230
So stay hydrated.

00:15:34.829 --> 00:15:38.490
And what happens when you add
water, it creates an alcohol.

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:42.810
Now, with most of these, it typically
happens under an acidic condition.

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:48.060
So very, you're very commonly you'll
see h plus written here, um, maybe

00:15:48.060 --> 00:15:54.930
H2SO4, or HNO3, some sort of strong
acid to tell you that we are trying

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:56.579
to create an acidic environment.

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:01.230
So when we're adding water, what
components do you have with it?

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:07.470
Well, it comes down to understanding what
the structure of water is, which is H2O.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:13.350
Now, if we were to break one of the
bonds of H2O, we can see the two parts.

00:16:13.355 --> 00:16:15.720
So let's just say if I
broke the bond right here.

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:21.870
Now with that, we can see that we have
a hydrogen as one part, and then we

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:25.770
also have an OH as our other component.

00:16:26.205 --> 00:16:30.015
So we are going to be adding a hydrogen
and an oh, and it's still gonna be

00:16:30.015 --> 00:16:36.315
governed by the Markovnikov's rule because
we have two separate different pieces.

00:16:36.855 --> 00:16:37.785
So same ideas.

00:16:37.785 --> 00:16:43.125
Before we're gonna go through this, we
can mark and draw our double bond, right?

00:16:43.130 --> 00:16:47.985
So this is where our doors are
going to be opened in our structure.

00:16:48.525 --> 00:16:50.205
We're going to open the bond.

00:16:50.625 --> 00:16:54.104
Hydrogen is gonna be added to
the side with more hydrogens.

00:16:54.464 --> 00:16:57.015
So let's illustrate our hydrogens.

00:16:57.104 --> 00:17:01.245
On the left hand side,
we have two hydrogens.

00:17:01.425 --> 00:17:03.104
On the right we have none.

00:17:03.584 --> 00:17:05.085
So where is hydrogen gonna go?

00:17:05.085 --> 00:17:08.204
It's gonna go on the left
side with more hydrogens.

00:17:09.194 --> 00:17:13.004
So when we draw that structure,
we will redraw it again.

00:17:13.484 --> 00:17:15.014
We'll draw the same carbons.

00:17:15.510 --> 00:17:19.950
But the only difference here is we're
going to be adding one hydrogen to the

00:17:19.950 --> 00:17:27.119
left and then our oh group on the right
hand side, creating an alcohol, right?

00:17:27.125 --> 00:17:31.530
So we are showing that tertiary
alcohol, um, where it's making tea,

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:33.720
butyl alcohol for our product here.

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:39.389
Uh, let's do some, uh, two more and
just so we can make sure we understand

00:17:39.510 --> 00:17:43.320
markovnikov rules and hydration reactions.

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:46.950
Now with this one, it's
the same general idea.

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:51.690
With the first one here, we are going
to be opening up that double bond.

00:17:51.750 --> 00:17:58.050
We are adding H and OH while our two
components that are gonna be added.

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:00.480
Now, where do they exactly go?

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:04.200
Well, we have to look at our structure
and count all of our hydrogens.

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:09.090
So if I look at the carbon on the
left hand side, well we can see we

00:18:09.090 --> 00:18:12.810
have one, two bonds, three bonds.

00:18:13.260 --> 00:18:16.920
So that means there is room for,
there's already, so that means

00:18:16.925 --> 00:18:19.530
there's one hydrogen currently here.

00:18:20.370 --> 00:18:24.240
Now if I go on the right carbon,
well we can see if we count all of

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:28.920
our bonds, we have 1, 2, 3, 4 bonds.

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:32.730
So that means that
there is zero hydrogens.

00:18:33.165 --> 00:18:34.425
On the right hand side.

00:18:35.055 --> 00:18:37.875
Okay, let, let me spell out
the word zero 'cause I keep

00:18:37.875 --> 00:18:38.675
thinking it looks like an OH.

00:18:38.955 --> 00:18:43.635
So we have zero hydrogens on the
right hand side, one on the left.

00:18:44.295 --> 00:18:50.835
So when we draw our new structure, when we
open up our double doors, we're going to

00:18:50.835 --> 00:18:53.865
be putting one hydrogen with its friend.

00:18:54.705 --> 00:18:58.695
So it's just gonna go over here on
the left with its other hydrogen.

00:18:59.159 --> 00:19:02.939
Then the oh is gonna go right
in the middle of our chain,

00:19:03.389 --> 00:19:05.129
um, away from that hydrogen.

00:19:05.135 --> 00:19:07.320
So hydrogen wants to go with more friends.

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:10.320
Now with some structures.

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:13.229
Markovnikov rule does not always apply.

00:19:13.695 --> 00:19:17.925
So, for example, with the following
structure, if I count all of my

00:19:17.925 --> 00:19:22.695
bonds, I can see that I have one
hydrogen on the left, or sorry, the

00:19:22.695 --> 00:19:24.675
top and one hydrogen on the bottom.

00:19:25.035 --> 00:19:27.285
So there is not a preference.

00:19:27.675 --> 00:19:31.725
It has to be whenever, usually it's
when there's branches involved.

00:19:32.230 --> 00:19:35.889
For other functional groups is
when you see that you're gonna have

00:19:35.889 --> 00:19:37.899
to add something special to it.

00:19:38.290 --> 00:19:42.340
Um, so you always wanna count your
hydrogens to see what's on both sides when

00:19:42.345 --> 00:19:45.280
you are going to go for your final result.

00:19:45.850 --> 00:19:49.210
So once again, we're gonna open
up those double doors and we're

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:51.870
gonna be adding a H and an OH.

00:19:52.610 --> 00:19:57.639
And for this structure, you could draw
either one, um, you could write it where

00:19:57.700 --> 00:20:00.700
it's H on the bottom, or OH on top.

00:20:01.065 --> 00:20:03.585
Because they're the exact
same structure, right?

00:20:03.585 --> 00:20:06.915
You can flip them and
they will be the same.

00:20:07.455 --> 00:20:09.015
So they are the same.

00:20:09.465 --> 00:20:09.735
Okay?

00:20:09.735 --> 00:20:11.505
So both exist.

00:20:11.925 --> 00:20:14.895
So it has to depend on really
what type of reaction you have.

